# what charCodeAt gives is a number only because it is NaN outside the text. Whoever builds the read has the code unit as an integer, with -1 outside, and comparisons and switches go by that. The reads are built by the rules for texts, without which every function here stays on numbers and texts
# each comparison is the condition of a Branch whose edge hands the char code on. A comparison whose edges hand nothing on is taken by the rules for texts, which are asked first and go by typed operations alone
# function isSpace(s, i) { if (typeof s !== "string" || !isInt32(i)) return 0; const c = s.charCodeAt(i); if (c === 32) return c; return 0; }
